e the books on the  backs of the poor and that's exactly  what we're now doing  the rationale is the need to pay  for the unprecedented and unexpected  costs  of the pandemic do you have confidence  that it will be  restored as the prime minister says it  will be when it's fiscally prudent to do  so  well it's not true that it's an economic  measure you see the thing about the 0.7  is that it goes up or down with the  performance of our economy so  as our economy has shrunk significantly  in the last year so the 0.7 has already  gone down by 2.9  billion this further cut is not really  an economic decision no one believes  that it's a political decision because  the amount of money we're talking about  is just one percent of the borrowing  over the last year  and there would have been other ways of  making those economies but to take it  out of the budget spent on helping  some of the poorest and most desperate  people in the world  in my view is a shameful act and you  know people  notice in africa all around the world at  the united nations they notice  that britain in the past has been a  development superpower really driving  forward  this tackling of international poverty  and they see today that  britain has simply relinquished that  role  forgive me for interrupting even with  the cuts the uk  still remains one of the most generous  in the world in terms of aid  well i'm not sure that's true actually i  mean the germans have said that because  of the pandemic they will exceed the 0.7  next year the french are making it clear  that they will stand by the 0.7 the  american administration  is seeking 15 billion dollars of extra  money to help  the poorest in this pandemic and and  britain having promised  indeed enshrined it in law that they  would  stand by the 0.7 is now arbitrarily  without any vote in the house of commons  stopping the 0.7 changing the target  from 0.7 to 0.5  and you know there was a statement an  urgent question that i was granted by  the 